ion just means the stuff being  radiated the stuff being emitted outward  not all radiation is bad for you  convection is what happens when you  cycle heat around in a fluid water  boiling in a pot does it a fancy oven  can do it with fans  technically all ovens do it it's kind of  unavoidable when there's a fluid around  convection ovens just do it better  it even happens with the plasma inside  the sun  but that's not the type you're seeing in  my exterior walls  that's conduction which requires  physical contact you can see where my  heating duct is because the duct is in  contact with my exterior wall  you can see where the studs are in my  studio because they physically connect  the exterior wall to the interior wall  notice that you can't see the studs when  both sides of the wall are inside the  house  you cannot see through walls with  infrared light you big creeper  oddly though you can't see through  windows either  [Music]  [Applause]  anyway the temperature change from  conduction can linger for longer than  you might expect  if i touch the wall for a bit and remove  my hand it takes time for that heat to  transfer into the surrounding wall and  air  the same goes for your footprints on the  floor the heat from your feet lingers  with everything you touch you're leaving  a little something behind  how that works is governed by the heat  equation this is a differential equation  which is just a fancy way of saying it's  a pattern that involves changes  this one relates changes in temperature  over time at a particular location to  differences in temperature between  nearby locations the bigger the  difference the faster the change and  vice versa it tells us how we'd expect  the heat to move around inside of a  solid body  but the specifics depend entirely on the  initial conditions  say we've got a uniformly heated rod  [Music]  since there's no temperature difference  between any nearby locations the heat  equation tells us temperature can't  change over time  in other words there's nowhere for the  heat to flow it just stays its current  temperature  a more interesting scenario would be two  uniform rods one warmer and one cooler  if the rods are brought into contact  we'd like to know what happens  our instincts tell us that the heat  would flow from the hot rod into the  cold rod and we'd be right but that  doesn't give us very much detail  applying the heat equation we can see  the fastest change starts at the contact  point over time the heat gradually  distributes throughout both rods until  they reach an equilibrium where they're  all the same temperature that all sounds  great but as experiment clone found out  a few days ago reality doesn't always  work out so nice  [Music]  both both of them have given up the  ghost  [Music]  hey  [Applause]  all i see is a big  [Music]  [Music]  as you can see the experiment didn't  work out as expected  i mean the propane flame was pretty  wicked but the cooler rod didn't  actually heat up that much it got a  little warmer at the contact point but  that's about it  we're gonna try but but even if we can't  fix this problem we need to at least  figure out why there's a problem because  frankly i hate it when i don't  understand something  our first thought was that maybe it'd be  easier to see on a big metal plate  instead  [Music]  it kind of worked  a large white spot formed as heat  quickly spread through the metal plate  but that spot is pretty difficult to see  when the rest of the plate is already  bright yellow  that means the plate was already hot  before we even started the experiment  awkward m had the good idea to bring the  plate indoors so we could cool it down  first  unfortunately by then we were running  out of propane plus experiment clone and  i were on the verge of a meltdown so  everybody decided we'd had enough  why was the plate already hot because it  was 85 degrees fahrenheit outside the  environment is our problem the  experiment isn't isolated 